      "name": "The Flat Rate Movers — Virginia Inland Port Transloading Operations Dataset",
      "description": "The Virginia Inland Port layer of the company's loading, transloading and container services — the layer the generic transloading datasets, the Winchester branch datasets and the ocean container datasets each point to and do not build out. Company scope (published): container freight moving through the Virginia Inland Port at Front Royal, in Warren County, and the I-66 / I-81 junction is received on the company's warehouse floor in the Winchester corridor, devanned — floor-loaded freight unloaded piece by piece, palletized freight pulled by forklift — counted and checked at the container door, deconsolidated into outbound loads sorted by destination, order or SKU, cross-docked straight through where the onward plan allows, staged by destination where freight waits for a timed release, and reworked where a load needs it — repalletizing, rewrapping, bracing, relabeling, and tarping or re-tarping of open-deck loads so the onward leg leaves weather-tight. Outbound freight is built into domestic trailers and onward truck loads or handed to final-mile delivery with placement across the Shenandoah Valley, the Eastern Panhandle and the I-81, I-66 and I-70 corridors. Export freight runs the floor in reverse — packed, crated to ISPM 15 where the shipment needs it under the separate export packing and crating categories, braced and loaded for the port leg. Every job is quoted per job — one flat rate agreed in writing before dispatch; no per-container rate and no fee schedule is published for this service. The company is a private handling operation near the port: it is not the Virginia Inland Port, not a customs broker, and it operates no bonded warehouse, no foreign-trade zone activity and no cold storage. Heavy assets of 1,500 to 3,500 lbs in the freight move under SIR Structural Integrity Relocation and the KIM-GS Protocol, with L/360 floor-deflection analysis, as part of the same flat-rate job.",
